  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="gratuity-content">
      <h2>Complete Guide to Gratuity Calculation in India</h2>
      <p>
        Gratuity is a statutory terminal monetary benefit payable by employers in India under the <strong>Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972</strong>. It rewards long-term, continuous service rendered by an employee upon resignation, superannuation, retirement, termination, or unfortunate death/disability.
      </p>

      <h2>Simple vs Advanced Gratuity Calculation</h2>
      <p>
        Depending on your requirements, you can choose between two calculation approaches:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Simple Gratuity Calculator:</strong> Designed for employees who already know their consolidated monthly wage (Basic + DA) and wish to quickly estimate their gratuity payout based on their years and months of service.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced Gratuity Calculator:</strong> Provides an itemized breakdown with separated Basic Salary, Dearness Allowance (DA), employment situation (Resignation, Retirement, Termination, Death, or Disability), daily wage basis, 15 days salary rate, and Section 10(10) tax exemption guidance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Statutory 15/26 Days Gratuity Formula</h2>
      <p>
        For all establishments covered under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972, gratuity is computed based on <strong>15 days of wages for every completed year of service</strong>, considering a 26-day working month:
      </p>

      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-1.5 font-mono">
        <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Gratuity = (Monthly Salary × 15 × Eligible Years) ÷ 26</div>
        <div class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Or: (Basic Salary + DA) × 15 × Eligible Years ÷ 26</div>
      </div>

      <h2>Service Year Rounding Rules (The 6-Month Rule)</h2>
      <p>
        The Payment of Gratuity Act provides specific rounding rules for incomplete years of service:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>More than 6 additional months (&gt; 6 months, i.e. 7 to 11 months):</strong> The incomplete year is rounded <strong>UP</strong> to the next full year.
          <ul>
            <li><em>7 years 7 months = 8 eligible years</em></li>
            <li><em>8 years 8 months = 9 eligible years</em></li>
            <li><em>10 years 7 months = 11 eligible years</em></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li><strong>6 additional months or less (&le; 6 months, i.e. 0 to 6 months):</strong> The fractional months are discarded and not rounded up.
          <ul>
            <li><em>7 years 6 months = 7 eligible years</em></li>
            <li><em>5 years 3 months = 5 eligible years</em></li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The 5-Year Continuous Service Eligibility Rule &amp; Exceptions</h2>
      <p>
        Under Section 4(1) of the Act, gratuity is legally payable only if an employee has completed <strong>at least 5 years of continuous service</strong> in cases of:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Normal Resignation</li>
        <li>Retirement or Superannuation</li>
        <li>Termination of employment</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50/80 p-4 dark:border-amber-900/60 dark:bg-amber-950/20 text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-1 text-amber-950 dark:text-amber-200">
        <strong>⚠️ Statutory Exception for Death or Permanent Disability:</strong>
        <p>
          The proviso to Section 4(1) of the Act explicitly mandates that the 5-year continuous service condition <strong>does NOT apply</strong> where the termination of employment is caused by <strong>death of the employee</strong> or <strong>disablement due to accident or disease</strong>. In such events, gratuity is payable immediately regardless of tenure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Tax Exemption Limit (Section 10(10))</h2>
      <p>
        Under Section 10(10) of the Indian Income Tax Act, the statutory tax-free exemption limit for gratuity is <strong>₹20,00,000 (₹20 Lakhs)</strong>. Gratuity received up to ₹20 Lakhs is 100% tax-free for covered private sector employees. Any excess amount received from an employer above ₹20 Lakhs is treated as taxable salary income.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">What is the difference between Simple and Advanced Gratuity Calculators?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            The Simple Calculator requires only your consolidated monthly salary and service period. The Advanced Calculator lets you itemize Basic Salary and DA, choose your specific separation reason (Resignation, Retirement, Termination, Death, Disability), and inspect daily wage and tax status breakdowns.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Why is monthly salary divided by 26 instead of 30?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            Under Indian labor law and Supreme Court precedents, a month contains 26 actual working days after excluding 4 weekly rest days (Sundays). Hence, daily wage is calculated as Monthly Wage ÷ 26.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Does service of 7 years and 6 months round up to 8 years?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            No. Under the Payment of Gratuity Act, only service exceeding 6 months in an incomplete year rounds up. Thus, 7 years 6 months equals 7 years, while 7 years 7 months rounds up to 8 years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Is gratuity payable if service is under 5 years?</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
            For normal resignation, retirement, or termination, 5 years continuous service is mandatory. However, in the event of death or permanent disability, this 5-year requirement is waived by law.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
